My office went on a bonding event to Penn’s Common Press, a cool facility that offers exploration and creative practice in the history, craft, and material culture of printing and bookmaking. I found it fascinating to trace the evolution of human communication, from the intricate letterpress to today’s digital media.
As the teacher was introducing the media types, I suddenly came to the realization that AI is the most modern iteration of a communication tool our society is currently embracing. While all forms of media serve to share knowledge, LLMs stand out as the first that can be truly proactive, highly personalized, and genuinely interactive!
The workshop primarily focused on the evolution of typefaces. In the 1400s, the very first Bibles featured typefaces that were quite challenging to read. However, as letterpress techniques advanced, designers began to pay more attention to typography and layout, as they have an important impact on comprehension. When newspapers and advertisements emerged, design truly became not only an art form but a strategy, with various tactics employed to capture readers’ attention.
